---
// Sources and licences of the Homer Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { LEMMA_FIXES, TAG_PATCHES } from "./annotation";
import { AGDT_COMMIT, AUTENRIETH_URL, LEXICA_COMMIT, PERSEUS_COMMIT, REFWK_COMMIT } from "./sources";

const patches = Object.entries(TAG_PATCHES).map(([key, parts]) => {
  const [poem, line, ...word] = key.split(" ");
  return { where: `${poem} ${line}`, word: word.join(" "), parts };
});
const fixes = Object.entries(LEMMA_FIXES);
---
<h2>Greek</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Homer, <i>Iliad</i>, book 1: David B. Monro and Thomas W. Allen (eds.), <i>Homeri Opera</i>, vol. 1–2 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 3rd ed. 1920)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The text, line numbers and paragraph breaks come from the Perseus Digital
    Library’s <a href="https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-greekLit">canonical-greekLit</a>
    edition <code>tlg0012.tlg001.perseus-grc2</code>, pinned to commit <code>{PERSEUS_COMMIT}</code>. Perseus’s encoding is
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>; this reader uses only the
    words, punctuation, line numbers and paragraph divisions. This is the edition the treebank annotated.
  </dd>
  <dt>Homer, <i>Odyssey</i>, book 1: A. T. Murray (ed.), <i>The Odyssey</i> (Loeb Classical Library, London: Heinemann, 1919)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. The Greek of Murray’s Loeb edition, Perseus’s <code>tlg0012.tlg002.perseus-grc2</code> from the
    same pinned commit and under the same licence. The treebank’s Odyssey annotates this text.
  </dd>
  <dt>Perseus Ancient Greek Dependency Treebank 2.1: lemmas and morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    Every word’s lemma and morphology come from
    the <a href="https://github.com/PerseusDL/treebank_data">Ancient Greek Dependency Treebank</a> (Perseus Digital
    Library, Tufts University; release 2.1, 2015, of annotation made in 2010–2011 by students and editors at Holy
    Cross and Tufts), <code>tlg0012.tlg001.perseus-grc1.tb</code> and <code>tlg0012.tlg002.perseus-grc1.tb</code>,
    pinned to commit <code>{AGDT_COMMIT}</code>.
    Licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/us/">CC BY-SA 3.0 US</a>. Only the lemma, the
    part-of-speech tag and the relation (which settles whether a conjunction subordinates) are used; the dependency
    trees are not shown. The treebank’s annotation was made by people, not by a tagger, and has the errors of such
    work: the sections below list the ones repaired.
  </dd>
  <dt>Morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    The treebank’s nine-position tags are converted to Universal Dependencies features by a fixed table
    (<code>packs/grc/build/agdt.ts</code>), following the conventions of the UD Ancient Greek Perseus treebank:
    the aorist is past tense without aspect, the imperfect past imperfective, the perfect past perfective. The
    treebank’s one form for middle and passive (<i>e</i>) is shown as “middle or passive”. Nouns whose lemma is
    capitalized are proper nouns. The build stops on any tag position it has no label for.
  </dd>
  <dt>Aligning the edition with the treebank</dt>
  <dd>
    Each printed word takes the annotation of the treebank token or tokens with the same letters, in order: the
    comparison ignores accents, breathings, elision marks, capitals and the form of sigma, because the treebank
    mistypes some forms (<i>ἄρῤ</i> for <i>ἄρʼ</i>). The treebank cites lines as it counts them; in Odyssey 1.95–269 it is
    off by a line, so the reader follows the text’s own line numbers. Where the treebank splits one printed word
    into two (<i>οὐδέ</i> as <i>οὐ</i> + <i>δέ</i>, <i>οὔτε</i>, <i>μηδέ</i>, 44 times in the Iliad, 5 in the Odyssey), the
    word shows both analyses, each against its own letters. Every printed word of both books matches; no word is
    left without annotation. The treebank’s “artificial” nodes (words it supplies to complete a construction, which
    the text does not print) are skipped.
    <details>
      <summary>Words repaired in the treebank’s annotation ({patches.length})</summary>
      <p>
        The treebank leaves 6 words of Iliad 1 and 10 of Odyssey 1 without lemma or tag, and gives some others a
        lemma that is not a word’s lemma. Each is repaired here, by hand, from the treebank’s annotation of the same
        word elsewhere. A tag has nine positions (part of speech, person, number, tense, mood, voice, gender, case,
        degree).
      </p>
      <ul>
        {patches.map(({ where, word, parts }) => (
          <li>
            {where} <i lang="grc">{word}</i>:
            {parts.map((part) => part && (
              <> {part.lemma ? <>lemma <i lang="grc">{part.lemma}</i></> : null}{part.lemma && part.postag ? ", " : null}{part.postag ? <>tag <code>{part.postag}</code></> : null};</>
            ))}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Lemmas corrected ({fixes.length})</summary>
      <p>
        These lemmas are not a dictionary’s headword, or carry a stray mark. Every token of the lemma is changed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {fixes.map(([from, to]) => <li><i lang="grc">{from}</i> becomes <i lang="grc">{to}</i></li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>A. T. Murray, <i>The Iliad</i> (Loeb Classical Library, London: Heinemann, 1924–1925) and <i>The Odyssey</i> (1919)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Perseus’s <code>tlg0012.tlg001.perseus-eng3</code> and <code>tlg0012.tlg002.perseus-eng3</code>,
    from the same pinned commit, under the same encoding licence as the Greek. Murray’s translation is prose. The
    reader does not number his lines.
  </dd>
  <dt>How the rows are cut</dt>
  <dd>
    A row is one page of the Loeb Greek (Perseus’s “card”): the Iliad’s 17 pages and the Odyssey’s 10, each named by
    the line it opens at (<i>Il. 1.33</i>, <i>Od. 1.44</i>). Murray’s English page opens at the same place and ends
    where the Greek page ends, on a sentence, so each row sets the Greek page beside its English. Perseus also marks,
    every fifth line, the point in Murray’s prose that reaches that line; those marks fall mid-sentence and are not
    used, and the English has no line numbers of its own. Within a row the Greek follows the paragraphs of the
    edition; the English follows Murray’s. Lines are numbered as in the Greek text, and a citation such
    as <i>Il. 1.33</i> or <code>#1.33</code> finds that line wherever it falls in a row.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Georg Autenrieth, <i>A Homeric Dictionary for Schools and Colleges</i>, translated by Robert P. Keep (New York: Harper, 1891)</dt>
  <dd>
    The dictionary of Homer’s own words, and the first the word panel shows. The 1891 book is public domain. The
    digital text is the Perseus Digital Library’s (<code>Perseus:text:1999.04.0073</code>,
    from <a href={AUTENRIETH_URL}>Perseus’s download of the work</a>); the file states no licence, Perseus says that its
    texts differ in copyright status, and Perseus refused automated requests for the page that would say which
    applies to this one, so no licence is claimed for the digital file beyond Perseus’s credit. Perseus has no
    versioned copy: the build checks the file against the hash it was made with and counts its entries, and Perseus
    may change the file.
  </dd>
  <dt>Henry G. Liddell and Robert Scott, <i>An Intermediate Greek-English Lexicon</i> (Oxford, 1889): the Middle Liddell</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain; the digital text is Perseus’s, <a href="https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-pdlrefwk">canonical-pdlrefwk</a>
    at commit <code>{REFWK_COMMIT}</code>, licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>.
  </dd>
  <dt>Henry G. Liddell, Robert Scott and Henry S. Jones, <i>A Greek-English Lexicon</i>, 9th ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1940): the LSJ</dt>
  <dd>
    The Perseus Digital Library’s <a href="https://github.com/PerseusDL/lexica">lexica</a> repository at
    commit <code>{LEXICA_COMMIT}</code> (27 files),
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Only the entries for words these two
    books use are kept, and those longer than about 14,000 characters of senses (<i>ἐπί</i>, <i>λόγος</i>) are cut after
    a whole sense, with a note saying how many senses are not shown.
  </dd>
  <dt>How a lemma finds its entries</dt>
  <dd>
    Each dictionary is searched for the treebank’s lemma, ignoring the marks that dictionaries add (length marks,
    hyphens in compounds, diaeresis) and case. A lemma that a dictionary spells with another accent is found by
    ignoring accents (<i>τε</i>, which the dictionaries accent <i>τέ</i>); the word panel then says under which
    headword it is listed. Every lemma of both books has at least one entry. The lexicon’s short gloss is the first
    translation in the first dictionary that has one; where an entry only refers the reader on (“see μάλα”) it is the
    gloss of the word it names.
  </dd>
</dl>
